* Roadmap

- **Why documentation matters with agents**: docs now have two readers, and a
  stale doc misleads an agent as much as a human
- **Docstrings and comments**: the imperative style, what a docstring contains,
  comments that explain why, and TODOs that do not rot
- **Architecture diagrams**: the four C4 levels, C4 diagrams in PlantUML, and
  class diagrams generated from the code
- **Docs as machine-consumable artifacts**: the Diataxis modes, a structure for
  two readers, self-documenting executables, and one home per fact
- **Maintaining docs**: docs in the same PR as the code, READMEs written by
  skills, and checks that catch drift
- **Project docs**: one doc per question, slot numbers, linkable requirements
  and decisions, and the flow from an idea to a merged PR

# Why Documentation Matters With Agents

* Two Readers: Humans and Agents
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/Tools_source.smd:915 '* Documentation as a Machine-Consumable Artifact'
// From: helpers_root/docs/documentation_meta/all.writing_docs.how_to_guide.md:35 '## Summary'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=58%}
- @Motivation@: a coding agent reads the docs of a repo before it edits a line
  - Docstrings, READMEs, and design docs enter its context
  - A human skims the same files to decide what to change

- @Comparison@:
  - _Human reader_: skims, reads the summary first, stops at the first answer
  - _Agent reader_: searches, loads whole files into a limited context, and
    follows each instruction literally
  - _Tool reader_: Sphinx, the IDE, and the linter parse docstrings and headers

- @Key idea@: the structure that makes a doc easy to skim for a human is the
  structure that makes it easy to search for an agent
::::
:::: {.column width=42%}

```graphviz
digraph TwoReaders {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.25;
    ranksep=0.45;
    rankdir=LR;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=11, margin="0.12,0.08",
          height=0.38];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75];

    // docs : green | readers : rose, coral, blue

    Code   [label="Code",
            fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    Doc    [label="Docstrings\nREADMEs\nDesign docs",
            fillcolor="#A9DDB0", color="#4F9A5C", fontcolor="#1F4E2E"];
    Human  [label="Human",
            fillcolor="#F6E1E8", color="#D98CA8", fontcolor="#6B2A44"];
    Agent  [label="Agent",
            fillcolor="#F6C6C6", color="#D98C8C", fontcolor="#6B2A2A"];
    Tools  [label="Tools",
            fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];

    Code -> Doc;
    Doc -> Human;
    Doc -> Agent;
    Doc -> Tools;
}
```
label=fig:two-readers
caption=One set of docs (green) describes the code (orange) for three readers: humans, agents, and tools

::::
:::

* Docs Rot Because Nothing Fails
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/Tools_source.smd:954 '* Generating and Maintaining READMEs with AI'
// From: helpers_root/docs/documentation_meta/all.where_to_add_material.explanation.md:6 '## Motivation'

- @Problem@: a doc is written once and rots, since no test fails when it goes
  stale
  - The code changes in every PR
  - Docstrings, READMEs, and diagrams change only when someone remembers

// TODO(gp): Check Fluri et al. (2007) and Wen et al. (2019): that the first
// finds that comments often do not change with the code, and that the second
// links code-comment inconsistencies to bugs. Both are recalled from memory
// and are not in the material read
- @Fact@: code and comments often do not change together _(Fluri et al.,
  2007)_, and an inconsistent comment is a known source of bugs _(Wen et al.,
  2019)_

- @Problem@: an agent makes a stale doc more expensive
  - A human may notice that a README is old and ignore it
  - An agent that reads a stale copy follows it, and copies it into new code
  - Two copies that disagree make the agent choose at random

- @Key idea@: treat a doc like code: it needs an owner, a review, and a check
  that fails when it is wrong

* A Running Example: Estimate Paper Quality
// From: research/Estimate_academic_paper_quality/README.md:1 '# Estimate Academic Paper Quality'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=58%}
- @Example@: a pipeline in this repo that turns a list of academic papers into
  a JSON report of candidate trading strategies
  - Four executables, one per stage: resolve, download, extract, and report
  - Built PR by PR, from a PRD and a set of design docs

- @Components@: every kind of doc in this lesson
  - _Docstrings_: REST style, with examples of the data
  - _READMEs_: one per executable and one for the dir
  - _Design docs_: C4 diagrams of the system and one doc per component
  - _Project docs_: a PRD with 20 requirements, a log of 38 decisions, a plan,
    and a progress log

- @Remark@: each example in this lesson comes from this project, so the picture
  stays the same from slide to slide
