# Docstrings and Comments

* Docstrings Describe the Interface
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.coding_style.how_to_guide.md:423 '#### General Conventions'
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.coding_style.how_to_guide.md:519 '#### Doctrings Content'

- @Definition@: a **docstring** is the string literal at the top of a module,
  class, or function that tells a caller what the code does and how to use it
  - PEP 257 fixes its place and its shape, not its markup _(Goodger and van
    Rossum, 2001)_
  - Markup conventions: ReST, Google, Numpydoc, and Epytext

- @Characteristics@: a docstring states the "what", not the "how"
  - The goal of the code, its inputs, and its outputs
  - Not a line-by-line translation of the code into English
  - A caller who needs the implementation reads the code

- @Intuition@: a docstring that describes the implementation changes at every
  refactoring
  - A docstring that describes the interface changes only with the interface

// TODO(gp): Check the claim that an agent often reads the docstrings of the
// functions it calls and not their bodies: it comes from experience with
// Claude Code, and it is not in the material read
- @Remark@: an agent that calls a function often reads its signature and its
  docstring, and not its body

* Descriptive Versus Imperative Style
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.coding_style.how_to_guide.md:437 '#### Descriptive vs Imperative Style'
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.coding_style.how_to_guide.md:542 '#### Comments Style'

- @Definition@:
  - _Descriptive style_: the docstring says what the function does, e.g.,
    _"Returns the path of the markdown"_
  - _Imperative style_: the docstring gives a command, e.g., _"Return the path
    of the markdown"_

- @Fact@: PEP 257 asks for the imperative style
  - _"It prescribes the function or method's effect as a command ("Do this",
    "Return that"), not as a description"_
  - Pylint and other QA tools favor it too

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Style", "Docstring", "Comment"),
  rows: (
    ("Descriptive (bad)", raw("Returns the path of a paper."), raw("# this computes the path")),
    ("Imperative (good)", raw("Get the path of a paper."), raw("# Compute the path of the paper.")),
  ),
  caption: "The same text in the two styles",
  col-widths: (1fr, 1.6fr, 1.8fr),
)
```

- @Key idea@: comments follow the same style as docstrings: imperative, a
  capital letter, and a final period

* Anatomy of a REST Docstring
// From: .claude/skills/coding.rules.md:761 '## Use Docstrings on Three Lines'
// From: .claude/skills/coding.rules.md:779 '## Use REST Style for Docstrings'
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.coding_style.how_to_guide.md:448 '#### Docstrings Style'
// From: research/Estimate_academic_paper_quality/epq_paths.py:124 'def get_markdown_path(output_dir: str, base_name: str) -> str:'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=52%}
- @Components@:
  1. A one-line summary in the imperative style
  2. An optional paragraph on the behavior, not on the implementation
  3. One `:param <name>:` for each parameter
  4. One `:return:` for the result
  - The quotes go on their own lines, even for a one-line docstring

- @Remark@: do not repeat what the type hints say
  - Write `:return: messages`, not `:return: a list of messages`
  - Do not write the default values
  - Then a list that becomes a set does not change the docstring
::::
:::: {.column width=48%}

```python
def get_markdown_path(
    output_dir: str, base_name: str
) -> str:
    """
    Get the path of the markdown of a paper.

    :param output_dir: dir of all the outputs
        of a run
    :param base_name: base name of the paper
    :return: `<output_dir>/paper_md/<BASE>.md`
    """
    return os.path.join(
        get_markdown_dir(output_dir),
        f"{base_name}.md",
    )
```

::::
:::

* Examples in Docstrings
// From: .claude/skills/coding.rules.md:849 '## Add Input/Output Examples to Docstrings When Non-Obvious'
// From: research/Estimate_academic_paper_quality/epq_types.py:135 'class Paper:'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=52%}
- @Problem@: a signature gives the type of the data, not its shape
  - `Dict[str, str]` does not say which keys exist
  - `str` does not say if a value is a path, a DOI, or a key

- @Solution@: add an example of the input or the output when the format is
  not obvious
  - Nested data, custom objects, and string formats
  - Transformations where the output looks unlike the input
  - Edge cases and special values

- @Pros@: an example is the fastest way for a human and an agent to see the
  format
  - It is also a test case that is waiting to be written
::::
:::: {.column width=48%}

```python
@dataclasses.dataclass(frozen=True)
class Paper:
    """
    A resolved, deduped paper.

    Example, the paper that two input
    lines (3 and 17) give:
        Paper(
            key="lazy prices|cohen",
            line_nos=(3, 17),
            year=2020,
            title="Lazy Prices",
            doi="10.1111/jofi.12885",
            ...
        )
    """
```

::::
:::

* Comments Explain Why, Not What
// From: .claude/skills/coding.rules.md:1045 '## Comment Chunks of Code to Explain Why'
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.coding_style.how_to_guide.md:626 '#### Comment Chunks of Code'
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.coding_style.how_to_guide.md:590 '#### Replace Empty Lines in Code with Comments'

- @Problem@: a wall of code hides its steps
  - The reader rebuilds the plan of the function line by line

- @Solution@: put one comment above each cohesive chunk of 3-5 lines
  - Say the intent of the chunk, or why it exists
  - Use the comment in place of an empty line, since an empty line also
    separates functions
  - Write down the invariants and the assumptions that the code keeps

- @Example@:
  ```python
  # Extract info.
  num_passed = info.get("log_num_passed", 0) or 0
  num_failed = info.get("log_num_failed", 0) or 0
  # Assemble result.
  res = {"passed": num_passed, "failed": num_failed}
  ```

- @Remark@: put the comment above the line, not at its end
  - The exception is an `if-elif-else`: comment each branch inside the branch

* Avoiding Distracting Comments
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.coding_style.how_to_guide.md:718 '#### Avoid Distracting Comments'
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.coding_style.how_to_guide.md:679 '#### Referring to an Object in Code Comments'
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.coding_style.how_to_guide.md:705 '#### Referring to Type of Vars in Code Comments'

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Smell", "Bad", "Good"),
  rows: (
    ("Restates the code", raw("# Print results."), "No comment"),
    ("Describes the how", raw("# Format the output by showing only the start and the end."), raw("# Format the output.")),
    ("Names a type", raw("# Filter the list of LLM prompts."), raw("# Filter the LLM prompts.")),
    ("Names an object that may change", raw("# Get the paths for ParquetDataset."), raw("# Get the paths for the Parquet dataset.")),
    ("Sits at the end of the line", raw("print(x)  # Say hello."), raw("# Say hello.") + " above the line"),
  ),
  caption: "Five comment smells and their fixes",
  col-widths: (1.1fr, 2fr, 1.7fr),
)
```

- @Key idea@: a comment that repeats the code goes stale at the first
  refactoring, while a comment that explains the intent survives it

- @Remark@: when a comment must name a code object, put it in backticks, e.g.,
  `` `df_tmp` `` or `` `get_md_colors()` ``
  - The backticks tell the object from the real-world thing, and a rename tool
    or a `grep` finds them

* TODOs and Commented-Out Code
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.coding_style.how_to_guide.md:1970 '#### Add Todos When Needed'
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.coding_style.how_to_guide.md:738 '#### Commenting Out Code'
// From: helpers_root/docs/coding/all.coding_style.how_to_guide.md:767 '#### If You Find a Bug or Obsolete Docstring/Todo in the Code'

- @Problem@: a TODO with no context goes stale
  - _"TODO: clean this up"_ does not say who, why, or when
  - Commented-out code with no reason looks like a bug or a leftover

- @Solution@: give each TODO an owner, a reason, and a link
  - `# TODO(gp): ...` makes the TODOs of one person easy to `grep`
  - Name the issue, e.g., `# TODO(Grisha): "Handle missing tiles" CmTask #1775.`
  - Mark the priority, e.g., `P1` for _"nice to have"_
  - Above commented-out code, say why it is off

- @Remark@: an obsolete docstring or TODO is a bug
  - `git blame` finds the author, and the fix goes in a PR or an issue

- @Techniques@: an agent turns TODOs into work
  - `coding.annotate_with_todoai` writes a `TODO(ai_gp)` for each rule
    violation in a file
  - `coding.todoai_gp` implements each `TODO(ai_gp)` and removes it

* Rules That Override Agent Defaults
// From: .claude/skills/coding.rules.md:1005 '## Add Comments Liberally'
// From: .claude/skills/coding.rules.md:1010 '## Do Not Remove Existing Comments'
// From: .claude/skills/coding.rules.md:824 '## Update Docstrings If Out-of-sync'
// From: .claude/skills/coding.fix_docstring/SKILL.md:2 'description: Fix Python Docstrings'
// From: .claude/skills/coding.fix_comments/SKILL.md:2 'description: Update docstrings and comments in a Python file without changing logic'

// TODO(gp): Check the claim that some harnesses tell the model to write few
// or no comments: it is recalled from memory, and the material only says
// "override any minimalist comment defaults"
- @Problem@: a coding agent has its own defaults for comments
  - Some harnesses tell the model to write few comments
  - When it rewrites a function, it may drop the comments that it finds
    redundant

- @Solution@: write the team style in a rule file that overrides the defaults
  - _"Add comments liberally"_: override any minimalist default
  - _"Do not remove existing comments"_: keep input-output examples, command
    outputs, and TODOs
  - _"Update docstrings if out of sync"_: fix a docstring that no longer
    matches the code

- @Example@: two small skills apply the rules to a file
  - `coding.fix_docstring`: rewrites the docstrings in REST style
  - `coding.fix_comments`: fixes docstrings and comments, not the logic
  - Both run on a small model (`model: haiku`), since the edit is local and
    easy to check

- @Key idea@: both skills share one check: _"the code logic and behavior are
  unchanged"_

# Architecture Diagrams

* Why Diagrams as Code
// From: helpers_root/docs/documentation_meta/all.architecture_diagrams.explanation.md:3 '## Summary'
// From: helpers_root/docs/documentation_meta/all.writing_docs.how_to_guide.md:51 '### Layout Rules'

- @Problem@: a diagram drawn in a GUI tool is an image
  - A reviewer cannot diff it
  - An agent cannot read it, nor update it when the code changes
  - It goes stale faster than the text around it

- @Solution@: write the diagram as text, in a fenced block of the doc
  - PlantUML, Graphviz, Mermaid, or TikZ
  - A script renders the block to an image, e.g., `render_images.py`
  - The source sits next to the prose, and changes in the same PR as the code

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Property", "Image from a GUI tool", "Diagram as code"),
  rows: (
    ("Diff in a PR", "No", "Yes"),
    ("Edit by an agent", "No", "Yes"),
    ("Control of the layout", "Full", "Automatic, from the relations"),
    ("Render on GitHub", "Yes", "Mermaid yes, PlantUML no"),
  ),
  caption: "An image and a diagram as code",
  col-widths: (1.3fr, 1.2fr, 1.6fr),
)
```

* The C4 Model: Four Levels of Zoom
// From: helpers_root/docs/documentation_meta/all.architecture_diagrams.explanation.md:25 '### Different Levels of Detail'
// From: .claude/skills/C4.rules.md:6 '## Open the Diagram Section with a C4 Refresher'

// TODO(gp): Check the year of Brown's C4 book or site to cite: the material
// gives only https://C4model.com
- @Definition@: the **C4 model** describes a software system with four
  diagrams, and each one zooms into one box of the level above _(Brown, 2018)_
  - C4 = Context, Container, Component, Code

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Level", "Shows", "Question it answers", "Audience"),
  rows: (
    ("C1 Context", "The system as one box, its users, and the external systems", "Who uses it, and what does it depend on?", "Everyone"),
    ("C2 Container", "Apps, services, scripts, and DBs that run or store data", "What is it made of, with which technology?", "Technical people"),
    ("C3 Component", "The modules or classes inside one container", "How is one container organized?", "Architects, developers"),
    ("C4 Code", "The classes and functions inside one component", "How is one component built?", "Developers"),
  ),
  caption: "The four levels of the C4 model",
  col-widths: (0.9fr, 2fr, 1.8fr, 1.1fr),
)
```

- @Remark@: draw C1 and C2 by hand, and generate C4 from the code, since at
  that level the code is the diagram

* Mapping C4 Onto the Repo
// From: helpers_root/docs/documentation_meta/all.architecture_diagrams.explanation.md:113 '### Mapping C4 and Code Structure'
// From: research/Estimate_academic_paper_quality/docs/02-design.architecture.md:19 '### C4 Refresher'

- @Problem@: a diagram whose boxes do not match the dirs and the files is hard
  to check against the code

- @Solution@: map each C4 level onto one level of the code tree
  - _Context_: a repo
  - _Container_: a top-level dir, or an executable
  - _Component_: a second-level dir, or a module
  - _Code_: the classes and functions of a file

- @Example@: Estimate Paper Quality
  - _C1_: the pipeline, a quant researcher, the bibliographic APIs, the paper
    hosts, and an LLM provider
  - _C2_: four stage scripts, a driver script, the infrastructure modules, and
    the output dir
  - _C3_: one design doc per component, `02.<N>-design.<component>.md`

- @Pros@: an agent can check a box against a path, and a reviewer can spot a
  box with no code behind it

* C4 Diagrams in PlantUML
// From: .claude/skills/C4.rules.md:33 '## Always Draw C4 Diagrams with PlantUML'
// From: .claude/skills/C4.rules.md:46 '## Start Every Diagram with the Same Header'
// From: .claude/skills/C4.rules.md:83 '## Fill the Technology Only for Mixed Technologies'
// From: helpers_root/docs/documentation_meta/all.architecture_diagrams.explanation.md:244 '#### Our Conventions'

- @Definition@: **C4-PlantUML** is a library of PlantUML macros for the C4
  elements: `Person`, `System`, `Container`, `Component`, `Rel`, and `Boundary`
  - The suffix `_Ext` marks what the team does not build

- @Comparison@: PlantUML and Mermaid for C4
  - _Mermaid_: puts the shapes on a fixed grid, so long descriptions render as
    one long column
  - _PlantUML_: lays out the shapes from the relations, so neighbors stay close

- @Techniques@: the conventions of the rule file
  - The same three header lines: `!pragma layout smetana`,
    `!include <C4/C4_Container>`, and `LAYOUT_TOP_DOWN()`
  - The level in the title, e.g., `title Estimate Paper Quality: Context`
  - A technology only when the elements mix technologies
  - `~-~-` for a literal `--`, since PlantUML reads `--text--` as strikethrough
  - A short C4 refresher before the first diagram, once per project

* A C1 Context Diagram
// From: research/Estimate_academic_paper_quality/docs/02-design.architecture.md:39 '### C1 (Context)'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=40%}
- @Example@: the context of Estimate Paper Quality
  - One system, one user, three external systems
  - Each arrow is labeled with what flows, and how

- @Remark@: the source of the diagram is 14 lines of text in
  `02-design.architecture.md`
  - An agent that adds an external API adds one `System_Ext` and one `Rel`
::::
:::: {.column width=60%}

```plantuml[width=100%]
!pragma layout smetana
!include <C4/C4_Context>
LAYOUT_LEFT_RIGHT()
title Estimate Paper Quality: Context
Person(user, "Quant researcher", "Gives a list of papers, reads the report")
System(epq, "Estimate Paper Quality", "Resolves, downloads, extracts, rates, reports")
System_Ext(bib, "Bibliographic APIs", "Semantic Scholar, Crossref, arXiv")
System_Ext(sites, "Paper hosts", "arXiv, SSRN, publisher sites")
System_Ext(llm, "LLM provider", "Reads the markdown of each paper")
Rel(user, epq, "Runs the stage scripts", "CLI")
Rel(epq, bib, "Looks up metadata and citations", "HTTPS")
Rel(epq, sites, "Downloads PDFs", "HTTPS")
Rel(epq, llm, "Extracts features with quotes", "HTTPS")
```
label=fig:epq-c1-context
caption=C1 view of Estimate Paper Quality: one user, the system, and three external systems

::::
:::

* UML Class Diagrams for the Code Level
// From: helpers_root/docs/documentation_meta/all.architecture_diagrams.explanation.md:374 '### UML Class Diagrams'
// From: helpers_root/docs/documentation_meta/all.architecture_diagrams.explanation.md:181 '### Generating Class Diagram'
// From: helpers_root/docs/documentation_meta/all.architecture_diagrams.explanation.md:158 '### Use Classes!'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Definition@: a **UML class diagram** shows the classes of a component, with
  their attributes, their methods, and their relations
  - `+` public, `-` private, `#` protected
  - Inheritance `<|--`, composition `*--`, aggregation `o--`, and dependency
    `..>`

- @Solution@: generate the diagram from the code, never draw it by hand
  - `create_class_diagram.sh <dir>` runs `pyreverse` and `dot` to make a PNG
  - Generate it again in the PR that changes the classes

- @Remark@: classes make C4 easier to draw
  - A class shows the public interface and the job of a group of functions
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

```plantuml[width=100%]
!pragma layout smetana
class BibSources {
  +lookup_s2(paper_id): BibRecord
  +lookup_arxiv(arxiv_id): BibRecord
  +search_crossref(query): List[BibRecord]
  +get_citation_count(...): CitationCount
}
class HttpSettings <<frozen>>
class BibRecord <<frozen>>
class CitationCount <<frozen>>
BibSources o-- HttpSettings
BibSources ..> BibRecord
BibSources ..> CitationCount
```
label=fig:epq-class-diagram
caption=Code view of the bibliographic clients: one service class and the frozen dataclasses it uses

::::
:::

# Docs as Machine-Consumable Artifacts

* Diataxis: Four Modes of Documentation
// From: helpers_root/docs/documentation_meta/all.diataxis.explanation.md:13 '## Diataxis: a Framework to Write Documentation'
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/Tools_source.smd:915 '* Documentation as a Machine-Consumable Artifact'

- @Problem@: a doc that mixes a lesson, a recipe, a spec, and a design essay
  serves none of its readers

// TODO(gp): Check the year to cite for Procida's Diataxis: the material gives
// only https://diataxis.fr/
- @Definition@: **Diataxis** splits the docs into four modes, each with one job
  _(Procida, 2017)_

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Mode", "Orientation", "Reader question", "Food analogy", "Suffix"),
  rows: (
    ("Tutorial", "Learning", "Teach me by doing", "Teach a child to cut vegetables", ".tutorial.md"),
    ("How-to guide", "Goal", "Help me do this task", "A recipe for lasagna", ".how_to_guide.md"),
    ("Reference", "Information", "Tell me the details", "An encyclopedia entry on tomatoes", ".reference.md"),
    ("Explanation", "Understanding", "Explain the design", "An essay on culinary history", ".explanation.md"),
  ),
  caption: "The four Diataxis modes and the file suffix of each one",
  col-widths: (1.1fr, 1fr, 1.2fr, 1.7fr, 1.2fr),
)
```

- @Remark@: a reference doc goes stale the fastest, so keep most of it in the
  code and its docstrings

* File Names Encode the Mode
// From: helpers_root/docs/documentation_meta/all.writing_docs.how_to_guide.md:283 '### Convention for file names'
// From: helpers_root/docs/documentation_meta/all.writing_docs.how_to_guide.md:105 '### Format of each doc'

- @Definition@: each doc is named
  `docs/{component}/{audience}.{topic}.{diataxis_tag}.md`
  - _Component_: the part of the system, e.g., `planning`, `tools`
  - _Audience_: who reads it, e.g., `all`
  - _Topic_: a verb-object phrase for a how-to guide, a name for a reference
  - E.g., `docs/documentation_meta/all.diataxis.explanation.md`

- @Pros@: the name answers three questions before anyone opens the file
  - A `find` or a `grep` on the name filters by mode or by audience
  - An agent picks the right file from a listing, without reading each one

- @Techniques@: a fixed shape inside each doc
  - One H1 with the title
  - A `## Summary` in bullets at the top
  - A table of contents that the linter builds
  - A `## Last review` section with names and dates

* Structure That Serves Both Readers
// From: helpers_root/docs/documentation_meta/all.google_technical_writing.how_to_guide.md:144 '### Summarize the Key Points at the Start'
// From: helpers_root/docs/documentation_meta/all.google_technical_writing.how_to_guide.md:136 '### State Document'S Scope'
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.Writing_Books_For_Humans_and_AI.md:75 '### Bullet Everything Possible'

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Convention", "Why a human needs it", "Why an agent needs it"),
  rows: (
    ("Summary in bullets at the top", "Decides in seconds if the doc is relevant", "Reads the top before it loads the rest"),
    ("Scope and non-scope", "Knows what the doc does not cover", "Does not extend the doc past its scope"),
    ("One idea per bullet", "Skims", "Finds one fact in one chunk"),
    ("Meaningful headers", "Navigates", "Links and greps a section by name"),
    ("One term per concept", "Does not confuse synonyms", "One term matches one search"),
    ("Diagrams as code", "Sees the structure", "Reads and edits the source"),
  ),
  caption: "Six conventions that help both readers",
  col-widths: (1.4fr, 1.6fr, 1.6fr),
)
```

- @Key idea@: an essay-shaped doc is expensive for a human to skim and
  expensive for an agent to search
  - Write nested bullets under clear headers

* Self-Documenting Executable Workflows
// From: helpers_root/papers/AIgentic_Development_System/part4.AI_optimized_development.tex:16 '\subsection{Self-Documenting, Executable Workflows}'
// From: research/Estimate_academic_paper_quality/estimate_paper_quality.py:4 'Run all the stages of the Estimate Paper Quality pipeline, in order.'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=52%}
- @Problem@: a how-to guide that lists shell commands goes stale when the
  commands change
  - An agent copies the stale command and fails, or invents its own

- @Solution@: make each executable its own doc
  - A task registry lists every operation: `invoke --list`
  - A task encodes the right way to run it, e.g., `invoke run_fast_tests
    --coverage` hides the Docker mounts and the `pytest` options
  - The docstring of a script holds a `# Usage Example` section, and
    `--help` prints it with `description=__doc__`

- @Pros@: one source of truth, found with no prior knowledge, and the same
  commands in every runnable dir
::::
:::: {.column width=48%}

\begingroup \scriptsize
```text
> estimate_paper_quality.py --help
  ...
  # Usage Example

  - Run the pipeline on a CSV with one
    paper per row:
  > estimate_paper_quality.py \
      --input papers_in.csv --output_dir out

  - Show the command of each action, with
    no execution:
  > estimate_paper_quality.py \
      --input papers_in.csv --output_dir out \
      --dry_run

  For a description of this file, see
  `.../estimate_paper_quality.py.README.md`
```
\endgroup

::::
:::

* CLAUDE.md as the Agent's Manual
// From: helpers_root/papers/AIgentic_Development_System/part4.AI_optimized_development.tex:53 '\subsection{Agent Instruction Manifests: Making the Repository Speak to AI}'
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/Lesson02.01_Skills_Organization_and_Management.smd:366 '* Agent Instruction Manifest: Definition'

- @Definition@: `CLAUDE.md` or `AGENTS.md` _(AGENTS.md, 2025)_ is a versioned
  doc whose first reader is a program
  - Architecture, conventions, canonical commands, and pointers to the rule
    files

- @Comparison@: a manifest and a classic doc
  - _Classic doc_: explains, for a newcomer who can ask questions
  - _Manifest_: instructs, for an agent that loads it at every session start
  - Each line of a manifest costs context in every session, so it holds
    pointers, not content

- @Characteristics@: a manifest is a doc, with the same failure mode
  - A stale command in `CLAUDE.md` runs in every session of every engineer
  - Review a change to the manifest like a change to the code

- @Remark@: agent instruction manifests, their layers, and their pointers to
  rules are covered in detail in Lesson 02.01

* One Home per Fact
// From: helpers_root/docs/documentation_meta/all.where_to_add_material.explanation.md:23 '### The Three Places'
// From: helpers_root/docs/documentation_meta/all.where_to_add_material.explanation.md:47 '### One Home per Fact'
// From: helpers_root/docs/documentation_meta/all.where_to_add_material.explanation.md:59 '### Direction of Links'

- @Problem@: the same fact ends up in a skill, a how-to guide, and a blog, and
  the copies drift
  - E.g., the naming pattern of a branch

- @Solution@: give each fact exactly one home, and link to it from everywhere
  else

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Place", "Reader", "Owns", "Never holds"),
  rows: (
    ("Skill or rule", "Agent", "How to do one step, conventions", "Why the flow exists"),
    ("How-to guide", "Human", "Order of steps, which skill, gates", "Copies of skill text"),
    ("Blog", "Public reader", "Story, trade-offs, lessons", "Anything a reader must follow"),
  ),
  caption: "Three places for new material, from the most precise to the most narrative",
  col-widths: (1.1fr, 1fr, 1.8fr, 1.6fr),
)
```

- @Techniques@: links go only from the narrative level to the precise level
  - Blog $\to$ how-to guide $\to$ skill or rule
  - A skill never depends on a blog, since a blog ages the fastest
  - `docs.add_content` adds text to the docs, and reports each chunk that is
    redundant or in conflict

# Maintaining Docs

* Update Docs in the Same PR
// From: helpers_root/docs/planning/all.plan_work.how_to_guide.md:300 '## Keep Docs in Sync'
// From: .claude/skills/project.rules.md:393 '## Map Each Event to the Doc to Update'
// From: .claude/skills/readme_dir.rules.md:32 '# Writing Conventions'

- @Problem@: _"update the docs later"_ means never

- @Solution@: update the docs in the PR that changes the code
  - The reviewer sees the code and the docs in one diff
  - A fixed map tells the author, or the agent, which doc each change touches

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Event", "Doc to update"),
  rows: (
    ("Change a module", "Its design doc, or the README of its component"),
    ("Change the args of an executable", "Its README, and every example that uses the args"),
    ("Make a decision", raw("03-decisions.md")),
    ("Finish a task", [#raw("04-plan.md") and #raw("07-progress.md")]),
    ("Add, rename, or remove a doc", [#raw("docs/README.md") and every doc that links it]),
    ("Add a term", raw("10-glossary.md")),
  ),
  caption: "Map from a change to the doc that the same PR updates",
  col-widths: (1.3fr, 2fr),
)
```

- @Techniques@: `/project.update_progress` drafts the updates of the plan, the
  progress log, and the decisions log

* READMEs for a File and a Directory
// From: .claude/skills/readme_file.rules.md:13 '# Overall Structure'
// From: .claude/skills/readme_dir.rules.md:11 '# Overall Structure'
// From: .claude/skills/project.rules.md:139 '## Say How a Doc Was Generated'

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Aspect", "File README", "Dir README"),
  rows: (
    ("Name", [#raw("<FILE>.README.md"), next to the file], [#raw("README.md") in the dir]),
    ("Answers", "What does this file do, and when do I use it?", "What does this dir do?"),
    ("Sections", "Summary, examples, inputs, outputs, architecture", "Summary, subdirs, files, executables, workflows, review roadmap"),
    ("Examples", "3-5 commands, from simple to complex", "The commands of each executable and workflow"),
    ("Written by", raw("/readme_file.create"), raw("/readme_dir.create")),
  ),
  caption: "The two kinds of README",
  col-widths: (0.9fr, 1.8fr, 1.8fr),
)
```

- @Characteristics@: a generated README starts with _"Generated by ..."_
  - A reader finds the skill that wrote it
  - A later run finds the skill to repeat

- @Remark@: the docstring of an executable points to its README, so `--help`
  and the README stay linked

* Generating READMEs With an Agent
// From: helpers_root/docs/planning/all.plan_work.how_to_guide.md:427 '## Document the Executables'
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/Tools_source.smd:954 '* Generating and Maintaining READMEs with AI'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=56%}
- @Key idea@: generation is cheap, and checking is the work
  - An agent writes a full README in one run
  - The value is a README that still matches the code

- @Techniques@: the order of the flow
  1. `/readme_file.create <executable>` for each executable
  2. `/readme_dir.create <dir>` for the dir, which reads the file READMEs
  3. A review of the diff, as for code
  4. A new run in each PR that changes an arg, or adds, renames, or removes a
     file

- @Remark@: the gates of the step
  - Every arg of the executable is in its README
  - The examples in the README run as written
::::
:::: {.column width=44%}

```graphviz
digraph ReadmeFlow {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.25;
    ranksep=0.32;
    rankdir=TB;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=11, margin="0.12,0.07",
          height=0.36];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    // code : orange | skills : blue | READMEs : green | review : rose

    Code    [label="Executables",
             fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    FileSk  [label="/readme_file.create",
             fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    FileRd  [label="<FILE>.README.md",
             fillcolor="#A9DDB0", color="#4F9A5C", fontcolor="#1F4E2E"];
    DirSk   [label="/readme_dir.create",
             fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    DirRd   [label="README.md",
             fillcolor="#A9DDB0", color="#4F9A5C", fontcolor="#1F4E2E"];
    Review  [label="Review the diff",
             fillcolor="#F6E1E8", color="#D98CA8", fontcolor="#6B2A44"];

    Code -> FileSk -> FileRd -> DirSk -> DirRd -> Review;
    Review -> Code [style=dashed, color="#8C8C8C",
                    label="  next change"];
}
```
label=fig:readme-flow
caption=The README flow: skills (blue) turn the executables (orange) into READMEs (green) that a human reviews (rose)

::::
:::

* Finding Stale Docs Incrementally
// From: research/ideas/draft.Use_agents_to_update_repo_doc.md:7 '## Core Idea'
// From: research/ideas/draft.Use_agents_to_update_repo_doc.md:51 '## Implementation plan'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=56%}
- @Problem@: a run of every README skill in every PR costs time and tokens,
  and touches READMEs whose code did not change

- @Solution@: update only the READMEs that are older than their sources
  - Find the last change of each source file and of its README, with `git` or
    an explicit tag
  - A README is stale if one of its sources changed after it
  - Run the README skill only on the stale ones
  - A `--no_incremental` flag forces a full update

- @Remark@: this is a research idea of the repo, not a tool that ships
  - Prior art to check, e.g., Context7 and readme-ai
::::
:::: {.column width=44%}

```graphviz
digraph StaleDocs {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.25;
    ranksep=0.32;
    rankdir=TB;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=11, margin="0.12,0.07",
          height=0.36];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    // input : orange | process : blue | output : green | review : rose

    Git     [label="git log of sources and READMEs",
             fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    Compare [label="Source newer than README?",
             fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Skill   [label="Run the README skill",
             fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Readme  [label="Updated READMEs",
             fillcolor="#A9DDB0", color="#4F9A5C", fontcolor="#1F4E2E"];
    Review  [label="Review in the PR",
             fillcolor="#F6E1E8", color="#D98CA8", fontcolor="#6B2A44"];
    Skip    [label="Skip",
             fillcolor="#FFFFFF", color="#9AA9B8", fontcolor="#243B53"];

    Git -> Compare;
    Compare -> Skill [label="  yes"];
    Compare -> Skip [label="  no"];
    Skill -> Readme -> Review;
}
```
label=fig:stale-docs
caption=Incremental update: compare the change dates (orange), run the skill only on stale READMEs (blue), and review the result (rose)

::::
:::

* Checks That Catch Drift
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/Tools_source.tools_reference.md:1811 '### Vale'
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/Tools_source.tools_reference.md:1892 '### lychee'
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/Tools_source.tools_reference.md:1877 '### markdown-link-check'
// From: research/ideas/draft.Comparison_of_Documentation_Agents.md:96 '## Key Examples'

- @Problem@: a reviewer reads the diff, not the docs that the diff makes wrong

// TODO(gp): Check that `doctest` and `pydocstyle` fit here: `pydocstyle` is in
// the references of the documentation-agents idea, and `doctest` is recalled
// from memory
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Check", "Tool", "What it catches"),
  rows: (
    ("Broken links", "lychee, markdown-link-check", "Pages that moved or were deleted"),
    ("Prose style and terms", "Vale", "A style rule not followed, a banned term"),
    ("Docstring shape", "pydocstyle, the repo linter", "A missing docstring, a wrong shape"),
    ("Examples that run", "doctest, a test that runs the README commands", "A dropped arg, a wrong output"),
    ("Match with the code", "An agent review with the rule files", "A docstring that no longer matches the code"),
  ),
  caption: "Checks that fail when a doc drifts",
  col-widths: (1.2fr, 1.6fr, 1.8fr),
)
```

- @Key idea@: an example that runs is the strongest check, since it fails
  exactly when the doc drifts

// TODO(gp): Check the claim that doc-drift checks in CI are still rare: the
// book map marks them as not covered by the material
- @Limitations@: none of these checks says if a doc is useful, and doc-drift
  checks in CI are still rare

* AI Documentation Tools: Capability Levels
// From: research/ideas/draft.Comparison_of_Documentation_Agents.md:66 '### Documentation Agent Capabilities'
// From: research/ideas/draft.Comparison_of_Documentation_Agents.md:55 '### Comparison of Documentation Agents'

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Level", "Capability", "Example behavior"),
  rows: (
    ("L0 Suggest", "Complete a partial docstring", "Autocomplete in the IDE"),
    ("L1 Generate", "Write one docstring", "The docstring of one function"),
    ("L2 Suite", "Document a whole class or module", "Docstrings for 50+ methods"),
    ("L3 Sync", "Update the docs when the code changes", "Fix a docstring after a parameter rename"),
    ("L4 Autonomous", "Generate, publish, and maintain", "An API docs site updated from the code"),
  ),
  caption: "Five capability levels of documentation agents",
  col-widths: (1fr, 1.6fr, 1.8fr),
)
```

// TODO(gp): Check the level of each tool: the material lists the tools and
// the levels, but it does not map one onto the other
- @Examples@:
  - _IDE assistants_: **GitHub Copilot** and **Tabnine**, at L0-L1
  - _Docs platforms_: **Mintlify** and **readme-ai**, at L2-L4
  - _Coding agents with skills_: **Claude Code** with `readme_file.create`, at
    L2-L3

- @Remark@: L3 is the level that matters for maintenance, and the hardest one
  to measure

* Evaluating Generated Docs
// From: research/ideas/draft.Comparison_of_Documentation_Agents.md:76 '### Documentation Evaluation Rubric'
// From: research/ideas/draft.Comparison_of_Documentation_Agents.md:96 '## Key Examples'

- @Problem@: generated docs read well, and fluent text can still be wrong
  - E.g., a docstring says that a function raises `ValueError`, and it never
    does
  - E.g., a usage example that does not run

- @Solution@: score the docs with a rubric that weighs accuracy the most

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Criterion", "Points", "What the reviewer checks"),
  rows: (
    ("Accuracy", "40", "Behavior, params, return, and exceptions match the code"),
    ("Readability", "25", "Clear prose, one style, good formatting"),
    ("Coverage", "20", "All public functions, classes, and methods"),
    ("Examples", "10", "Present, correct, and runnable"),
    ("Maintainability", "5", "Easy to generate again after a change"),
  ),
  caption: "A 100-point rubric for documentation",
  col-widths: (1.2fr, 0.7fr, 3fr),
)
```

- @Techniques@: tests that catch hallucinated docs
  - Run each example in a docstring or a README
  - Rename a parameter, and check that the agent updates the docstring and
    the examples

# Project Docs

* One Doc per Question
// From: .claude/skills/project.rules.md:21 '## Use One Doc per Question'
// From: helpers_root/docs/planning/all.plan_work.how_to_guide.md:98 '## Plan Documents'

- @Problem@: one big project doc mixes requirements, design, plan, and status,
  and each part goes stale at its own speed

- @Solution@: a small set of docs in `docs/`, each one answering one question

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Doc", "Question it answers"),
  rows: (
    (raw("01-prd.md"), "What must the product do, and not do?"),
    (raw("02-design.architecture.md"), "How is the whole code built?"),
    (raw("02.<N>-design.<component>.md"), "How is one component built?"),
    (raw("03-decisions.md"), "Why is it built this way?"),
    (raw("04-plan.md"), "What to build, in which order, by when?"),
    (raw("07-progress.md"), "What is done, learned, and next?"),
    (raw("08-how_we_work.md"), "How does the team work on it?"),
  ),
  caption: "The core project docs, one question each",
  col-widths: (1.6fr, 2fr),
)
```

- @Remark@: start with the plan, the progress log, and the meta-doc, and add
  the others only when needed
  - Writing the PRD and the plan is covered in detail in Lesson 12.01

* Slot Numbers and Linkable IDs
// From: .claude/skills/project.rules.md:43 '## Name the Docs with a Slot Number'
// From: .claude/skills/project.rules.md:178 '## Link Requirements and Decisions'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=62%}
- @Definition@: a doc is named `<slot>-<role>.md`, and a slot is the same in
  every project
  - `ls docs/` lists the docs in reading order
  - A missing doc leaves a gap: the other slots do not shift
  - `02.1`, `02.2`, ... are the component designs, in pipeline order

- @Definition@: each requirement and each decision is an H3 with a stable id
  - `` ### `R3` (Bibliographic Source) `` in `01-prd.md`
  - `` ### `D27` (The Pipeline Is Split Into One Executable Per Stage) `` in
    `03-decisions.md`
  - Never reuse or renumber an id

- @Techniques@: refer to an id with a link to its header, never with a bare id
  - The design doc, the plan, and the PR description link the same anchor
  - An agent follows the link, and does not guess which `R3` is meant
::::
:::: {.column width=38%}

\begingroup \scriptsize
```text
docs/
  README.md
  01-prd.md
  02-design.architecture.md
  02.1-design.pipeline.md
  02.2-design.resolver.md
  02.3-design.bib_sources.md
  02.4-design.fetcher.md
  02.5-design.extractor.md
  02.6-design.rater.md
  02.7-design.report.md
  03-decisions.md
  04-plan.md
  07-progress.md
```
\endgroup

::::
:::

* Decision Records
// From: .claude/skills/project.rules.md:373 '## Record Every Non-Obvious Choice'
// From: research/Estimate_academic_paper_quality/docs/03-decisions.md:703 '### `D27` (The Pipeline Is Split Into One Executable Per Stage)'

// TODO(gp): Check the reference for ADRs: Nygard's post "Documenting
// Architecture Decisions" (2011) is recalled from memory, and the material
// only says "ADRs (Architecture Decision Records)"
- @Definition@: an **architecture decision record** (ADR) states one choice:
  the decision, the reason, the alternatives, and the consequences _(Nygard,
  2011)_
  - Status: `proposed`, `accepted`, or `superseded`

- @Example@: `D27` of Estimate Paper Quality, shortened
  \begingroup \scriptsize
  ```text
  ### `D27` (The Pipeline Is Split Into One Executable Per Stage)
  - Decision: one executable per stage, linked only by files on disk
  - Why: the stages differ in dependencies, runtime, and schedule
    - `download` needs Docker and Chrome, `extract` needs the LLM client
    - `rate` runs every month, `download` runs once per paper
  - Alternatives: one script with actions, or a driver that runs the
    stages in one process
  - Consequences: each script holds the loop of its stage
  ```
  \endgroup

// TODO(gp): Check the claim that an agent reverts to a rejected design when
// there is no decision log: it is an illustration, not from a source
- @Pros@: the log answers the question that the code cannot answer: _"why not
  the obvious alternative?"_
  - With no log, an agent may "simplify" the code back to the rejected design

* The Flow of Project Docs
// From: helpers_root/docs/planning/all.plan_work.how_to_guide.md:568 '# Software Project Documents'

```graphviz
digraph DocFlow {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.30;
    ranksep=0.35;
    rankdir=LR;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=11, margin="0.12,0.07",
          height=0.36];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    // specs : orange | design : blue | plans : teal | PR : rose | logs : green

    Idea   [label="idea.md",
            fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    Prd    [label="01-prd.md",
            fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    Arch   [label="02-design.\narchitecture.md",
            fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Comp   [label="02.N-design.\n<component>.md",
            fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Plan   [label="04-plan.md",
            fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
    Spec   [label="06-spec.\n<PR_ID>.md",
            fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
    Pr     [label="PR merged",
            fillcolor="#F6E1E8", color="#D98CA8", fontcolor="#6B2A44"];
    Prog   [label="07-progress.md",
            fillcolor="#A9DDB0", color="#4F9A5C", fontcolor="#1F4E2E"];
    Dec    [label="03-decisions.md",
            fillcolor="#A9DDB0", color="#4F9A5C", fontcolor="#1F4E2E"];

    Idea -> Prd -> Arch -> Comp -> Plan -> Spec -> Pr;
    Pr -> Prog;
    Pr -> Dec;
    Pr -> Comp [style=dashed, color="#8C8C8C",
                label="  same PR"];
}
```
label=fig:project-doc-flow
caption=Project docs from idea to merged PR: specs (orange), design (blue), plans (teal), and the logs (green) that each merged PR (rose) updates

- @Intuition@: each doc is the input of the next one, so a stale doc upstream
  misleads every step downstream

- @Key idea@: the merged PR closes the loop: it writes back what was done in
  the progress log, and why in the decisions log

* Summary

- @Key idea@: docs now have two readers, a human who skims and an agent who
  follows every line, so a stale doc does more damage than before
- Docstrings describe the interface in the imperative style, with examples of
  the data, and comments explain why, not what
- C4 diagrams zoom from the context to the code, live as PlantUML next to the
  prose, and map onto the dirs and the files of the repo
- Diataxis modes, file names, summaries at the top, and one home per fact make
  a doc easy to skim for a human and easy to search for an agent
- Update the docs in the PR that changes the code, let skills write the
  READMEs, and rely on checks that fail, such as links and runnable examples
- A project keeps one doc per question, with stable ids for requirements and
  decisions, so every doc, PR, and agent links the same anchor

